## FlowLeap CLI — Agent Rules

Rendered by flowleap-cli v0.0.0-test from its bundled agent skills. Refresh after a CLI upgrade with `flowleap skills update`.

FlowLeap is a CLI for the FlowLeap Patent AI backend: patent/USPTO/OPS/academic/NPL/legal/citation search plus an agent-first tools facade.

- Add `--json` to every command for stable machine-readable output.
- Run `flowleap --json doctor` first to verify config, auth, and backend reachability.
- Authenticate with `flowleap auth login`, or set `FLOWLEAP_API_KEY`/`FLOWLEAP_TOKEN` for headless use.
- If an error carries a `providerKeysHint` (`provider_keys_required` / `provider_keys_invalid` / `trial_budget_exhausted`), that office is gated on a patent-data key only the user can add (free from the office). It is a user-action stop, never an exhausted route: do not retry, do not invent keys, and do not substitute web-scraped patent data for that office — searches or single-document reads. `trial_budget_exhausted` means today's shared trial data budget is spent; it lifts at the hint's `resetsAt`, and the user's own free keys lift it permanently. Ask the user to add the key on the FlowLeap Patent-data keys page (the hint's `keysPageUrl`, when present) or to run `flowleap setup`; never ask for the key value in the chat.
- With one office gated and the other live, deliver the live office's results in full, name the gap as a missing-key gap (never as limited coverage), never narrow a prior-art or FTO scope to the keyed office, and ask for the key at the end. `patstat`, `academic`, and `npl` stay live keyless — offer them as different data, not as a substitute. Only that explicit error means gated: empty results, truncated payloads, and 5xx keep the normal retries.
- Discover every backend tool with `flowleap tools list`; run one with `flowleap tools run <name>`.

### Command Reference

#### flowleap — Start here for patent research with FlowLeap.

```bash
flowleap --json doctor
flowleap skills install
flowleap upgrade --check
flowleap skills update
```

#### flowleap-academic — Search scholarly papers in Semantic Scholar and arXiv, with source and publication-year filters, as prior art or technical background.

```bash
flowleap academic search <query> [flags]
```

#### flowleap-auth — Sign in the FlowLeap command-line tool: device-flow login, personal API tokens (fl_pat_) for agents and CI, status checks and logout.

```bash
flowleap auth login
flowleap auth create-token --name my-agent --store
flowleap auth tokens
flowleap auth revoke-token <id>
flowleap auth status
flowleap auth logout
```

#### flowleap-citation — Read the citations that USPTO examiners made in office actions: the references cited against one application, the later applications that cite a document, citation statistics, and the X-category references that an examiner held to destroy novelty.

```bash
flowleap --json citation search 16123456 --size 20
flowleap --json citation forward US10123456 --size 20
flowleap --json citation stats 16123456
flowleap --json citation novelty 16123456
```

#### flowleap-keys — Set up and check the patent-office data keys (EPO OPS key and secret, USPTO ODP key) of the FlowLeap command-line tool: key status, live validation, the gate codes, and the hand-over to the human for the setup wizard.

```bash
flowleap --json keys list
flowleap --json keys test
flowleap --json doctor
flowleap --json keys set epo --key <consumer-key> --secret <consumer-secret>
```

#### flowleap-legal — Read patent law from the office's own page (the MPEP, 35 USC and 37 CFR, the EPC and the EPO Guidelines, the PCT rules) and quote it with its URL and edition.

#### flowleap-npl — Search non-patent literature (journal articles, conference papers, theses, standards and preprints in OpenAlex) with year, open-access and publication-type filters, and identify a paper that an examiner cited from its DOI or citation string.

```bash
flowleap --json npl "perovskite solar cell stability" --limit 5
flowleap --json npl "CRISPR delivery" --from-year 2020 --to-year 2024 --open-access
flowleap --json npl "transformer attention" --type preprint
flowleap --json npl "hydraulic accumulator bypass" --to-year 2022 --type dissertation
flowleap --json npl-work 10.1038/nature14539
flowleap --json npl-work "SMITH J: 'Hydraulic actuator control', IEEE TRANS., 2015"
flowleap --json ops biblio EP3000000A3 --resolve-npl
```

#### flowleap-ops — Read authoritative EPO data for a known publication: bibliography, family members, legal status and abstract for documents worldwide, claims and description for EP and WO, and US claims in English, plus EPO search in CQL and EP Register search by representative or opponent.

```bash
flowleap ops search --cql <query> [flags]
flowleap ops register-search --cql <query> [--start 1] [--end 25]
flowleap ops biblio <doc>
flowleap ops claims <doc> --lang en
flowleap ops description <doc> --lang en
flowleap ops family <doc>
flowleap ops legal <doc>
flowleap ops abstract <doc>
flowleap ops register-biblio <EP-doc>
```

#### flowleap-patent — Search European and worldwide patents in the EPO database with a CQL query built from the invention: the field reference, the discriminating-term rule, and a count check before each full search.

```bash
flowleap patent search --query <query> [flags]
flowleap patstat query "SELECT symbol, title FROM flowleap.cpc_scheme WHERE title ILIKE '%photovoltaic%' ORDER BY symbol LIMIT 15" --question "candidate CPC codes for photovoltaics"
```

#### flowleap-patstat — Count and analyse patent filings in the PATSTAT database (a twice-yearly EPO snapshot): an applicant's portfolio, counts by applicant, CPC class, office, year, family or grant status, priority and continuation chains, decoded legal events, the CPC codes that a concept uses, and the reach of an extended family, with the database edition cited on every number.

```bash
flowleap --json tools run patstat_portfolio applicant="<applicant name>"
flowleap --json patstat portfolio "Siemens AG" --from-year 2015 --to-year 2023
flowleap patstat docs --section examples
flowleap patstat query "SELECT office, COUNT(DISTINCT family_id) AS inventions FROM flowleap.applications a JOIN flowleap.applicants ap ON ap.application_id = a.application_id WHERE UPPER(ap.name) LIKE 'SIEMENS%' GROUP BY office ORDER BY inventions DESC" --question "where does Siemens hold the most inventions?"
```

#### flowleap-patstat-graph — Trace the relationships around one patent or applicant in the PATSTAT database: who cites a patent (examiner or applicant), how two patents connect through citations or families, where a family has coverage, an applicant's co-applicants with their top CPC classes and countries, the CPC codes for a technology keyword, and the landscape of one CPC area (top applicants, filing trend, grant rate, new entrants).

```bash
flowleap patstat graph resolve EP3477840
```

#### flowleap-shared — Common rules for every FlowLeap skill: the tool to call for each method in the FlowLeap Patent AI app and in chat clients, what to do when a patent office needs a data key, which offices each text tool covers, and, for the FlowLeap command-line tool, sign-in, settings and output formats.

```bash
flowleap config set base-url https://api.flowleap.co
flowleap config get base-url
flowleap --json health
flowleap --json health api
flowleap upgrade --check --json
```

#### flowleap-tools — Discover and run FlowLeap tools from the command line through one tool interface (search_patents, get_bibliography, get_claims, get_patent_summary, compare_patents and more) instead of provider-specific commands.

```bash
flowleap --json tools list
flowleap --json tools describe get_bibliography
flowleap --json tools openapi
flowleap --json tools run get_bibliography patent_number=EP1000000
flowleap --json health api
```

#### flowleap-uspto — Search US patent and application records (USPTO Open Data Portal) and read the file wrapper: grants, applications, continuity chains, prosecution transactions, assignments, foreign priority, patent term adjustment, attorney of record, and the text of office actions and other file-wrapper documents.

```bash
flowleap --json uspto search --query 'applicationMetaData.inventionTitle:"machine learning"' --limit 5
flowleap --json uspto grant 11800000
flowleap --json uspto application 16123456
flowleap --json uspto continuity 16123456
flowleap --json uspto transactions 14412875
flowleap --json uspto assignments 14412875
flowleap --json uspto foreign-priority 14412875
flowleap --json uspto adjustment 14412875
flowleap --json uspto attorney 14412875
```

### Workflow Triggers

- **persona-ip-analyst** — Act as an IP analyst for technology-landscape mapping, portfolio assessment, white-space identification and filing-trend analysis. Use it when the user asks for an IP analyst's view, a landscape map, an assessment of a competitor's portfolio, or patent filing trends.
- **persona-patent-attorney** — Act as a patent attorney for prior-art search, claim-scope analysis and freedom-to-operate clearance. Use it when the user asks for a patent attorney's view, a review of prior art, an analysis of claim scope, or FTO clearance for a product.
- **persona-researcher** — Act as a researcher who explores academic literature and patents side by side to show what is published and what is protected. Use it when the user asks for a researcher's view, a survey of a technology across papers and patents, or the gaps between academic work and filed patents.
- **persona-startup-founder** — Act as an adviser to a startup founder for novelty checks, freedom-to-operate scans and the monitoring of competitor patents. Use it when the user asks to check a startup's patent position, whether an idea is already patented, or which competitor patents matter before building.
- **recipe-academic-literature-review** — Compare published research with filed patents for a technology: a literature search in Semantic Scholar, arXiv and OpenAlex beside a matching patent search, with the result organised around the gap between what is published and what is protected, not as a ranked novelty list. Use it when the user asks for a literature review, a state-of-the-art survey, or a comparison of academic research with patents.
- **recipe-audit-report** — Produce an auditable record of AI-assisted patent research: a reproducible log of each tool call with its exact input and saved answer, the data source of every finding, a dated check of each patent's status, a re-run of the load-bearing calls, and an AI-use disclosure for internal or filing-related records. Use it when the user asks for an audit trail of patent research, a disclosure of AI assistance, or a verifiable write-up of the method.
- **recipe-claim-analysis** — Break a patent's claims into elements, keywords and search queries, with the claims, abstract, bibliography, description and family as context. Use it when the user asks what a patent claims, to break claims into elements, or to interpret claim scope in the light of the specification.
- **recipe-claim-drafting** — Draft patent claims on the basis of the prior art: find the closest art, study its claim language, break the invention into old and novel elements, draft independent and dependent claim sets, check the formal rules of the EPC, the EPO Guidelines, 35 USC 112, 37 CFR 1.75 and the MPEP, and search again from each novel element until the closest art lacks one and that element is not obvious over the closest art combined with one other document. Use it when the user asks to draft or improve claims, to write independent and dependent claims, or to test draft claims against known art and formal requirements.
- **recipe-custom-dashboard** — Turn verified patent data into a self-contained HTML dashboard with charts: the agent saves each tool answer unchanged as a JSON file, a small zero-dependency Node program checks each file against its tool input and computes every number from the files, and a footer gives the tool, input, dataset identity and save method of each source. Without Node it reports the verified numbers as the tools returned them. Use it when the user asks for a dashboard, chart or visual report of patent data, such as a company's portfolio, filing trends across competitors, a technology landscape or white-space map, or citation impact.
- **recipe-find-better** — Find closer prior art than the examiners cited against a granted patent: read the Examiner Baseline (every document cited across the family, by office), run three logged search tracks (backward citations two levels deep, inventor and author networks, classifications with discriminating terms), and score the examiners' best art and the new art on the same claim elements as 'disclosed n of m', each row quoted from a tool result. Use it when the user asks whether earlier or closer prior art exists than the examiners cited, or what the examiners missed on a granted patent. Not for a novelty search before filing (recipe-prior-art-search) or for an invalidity case (recipe-invalidity-analysis).
- **recipe-freedom-to-operate** — Freedom-to-operate clearance for a product or technology: search queries for each product feature, a recall search for blocking patents in the EPO and USPTO databases, checks of legal status, designated states and family for each candidate, and an all-elements check of each independent claim of each live candidate, with unread claims and missing-key offices kept as open gaps. Use it when the user asks whether a product or technology can launch without infringing, or asks for an FTO or clearance search.
- **recipe-infringement-charting** — Build an element-by-element infringement claim chart: check that the patent is in force where it matters, break the asserted claims into elements, extract evidence about the accused product (with OCR of datasheets and manuals), and map each element with literal and doctrine-of-equivalents columns, each cell tied to its evidence. Use it when the user asks whether a product infringes a patent, wants a claim chart or evidence-of-use chart, or needs an element-by-element infringement map.
- **recipe-invalidity-analysis** — Build an invalidity case against a patent: fix the critical date, build the Fallback Ladder (each dependent claim with the limitation it adds) from the claims, search patent and non-patent prior art for every element and every rung, chart it with X/Y/A tags, quoted passages and Fallback Coverage counts, then write a Round Report and run one search round for each expert answer. Use it when the user asks to invalidate a patent, to find 102/103 or Art. 54/56 prior art against granted claims, or to assess how strong a patent is.
- **recipe-invention-disclosure** — Turn a conversation with an inventor into a complete invention disclosure form: structured technical capture, a bar-date check, a novelty check across patents and literature with the difference from each close hit recorded, landscape context and a filing recommendation. Use it when the user asks to document an invention, to create an invention disclosure, or to run a novelty check before filing.
- **recipe-maintenance-fees** — Check the maintenance-fee status and deadlines of US patents: the 3.5, 7.5 and 11.5-year windows from the grant date, the fees actually paid according to the USPTO transaction events, and the next deadline with its surcharge dates. Use it when the user asks whether maintenance fees are due or paid, when the next fee is due, whether a US patent is still in force for fees, or for a fee check across a portfolio. Dates and status only, never fee amounts.
- **recipe-office-action-response** — Analyse the office action of one EP or US application: find the action type and posture, read the newest examiner action, list each objection with its legal basis, interpret the disputed claim terms, read each cited document at the passages the examiner relied on, run the novelty and inventive-step tests, and write an analysis with response options and deadlines for the attorney, with each statement about a cited document tied to its source passage. Use it when the user asks to analyse or respond to an office action, an EPO communication, search opinion or search report, or a USPTO rejection, or when a routine names an application and a new office-action event.
- **recipe-patent-landscape** — Map the patents of a technology area: a scoped search in the EPO and USPTO databases, the key players, filing trends, the jurisdiction split and white space found by CPC class against year, each figure with its counting basis and source. Uses the FlowLeap Data analytics and PATSTAT tools when they exist, and live search counts when they do not. Use it when the user asks to map a technology area, to find who patents in it, or to report filing trends and white space.
- **recipe-patent-to-report** — Write a complete dossier on one patent: bibliography, abstract, claims, description, family with the status of each member, legal status, prosecution timeline, citations, the US family chain, figures and related art, in one structured report with its sources and gaps. Use it when the user asks for a full profile, dossier or report on a specific patent.
- **recipe-prior-art-search** — Search for prior art before filing or against a published patent: synonym rows for each element, patent searches in the EPO and USPTO collections with a classification-only track and a citation track, a literature search (OpenAlex first), a recall check against the Examiner Baseline, and X/Y/A tags on the closest hits, each statement tied to the tool result it came from. Its search method is the one the other searching methods use. Use it when the user asks to find prior art for an invention, to run a novelty search, or to check what already exists before filing.
